Here's exactly how it works, traced through the source.

---

## 1. `app.use(...)` — registering the middleware

`app.use` is assigned in the constructor of `HonoBase`:

**`src/hono-base.ts` ~line 153–165**
```ts
this.use = (arg1: string | MiddlewareHandler<any>, ...handlers: MiddlewareHandler<any>[]) => {
  if (typeof arg1 === 'string') {
    this.#path = arg1          // explicit path like '/api/*'
  } else {
    this.#path = '*'           // no path → match everything
    handlers.unshift(arg1)
  }
  handlers.forEach((handler) => {
    this.#addRoute(METHOD_NAME_ALL, this.#path, handler)  // ← key call
  })
  return this as any
}
```

`METHOD_NAME_ALL` is the string `"ALL"`. Each handler goes through `#addRoute`:

**`src/hono-base.ts` (private method)**
```ts
#addRoute(method: string, path: string, handler: H): void {
  method = method.toUpperCase()
  path = mergePath(this._basePath, path)
  const r: RouterRoute = { basePath: this._basePath, path, method, handler }
  this.router.add(method, path, [handler, r])   // ← stored in the router trie
  this.routes.push(r)
}
```

**Nothing is composed yet.** `app.use(...)` just inserts `(handler, routeRecord)` pairs into the router (a trie/regexp tree, depending on which `Router` implementation you chose). The chain doesn't exist as a linked list — it's reconstructed per-request from the match result.

---

## 2. Dispatch — building and running the chain at request time

Every incoming request enters through `fetch` → `#dispatch`:

**`src/hono-base.ts` ~line 225–260 (the `#dispatch` method)**
```ts
const path = this.getPath(request, { env })
const matchResult = this.router.match(method, path)   // ← pull matched handlers

const c = new Context(request, { path, matchResult, ... })

// Fast path: only one handler matched, skip compose overhead
if (matchResult[0].length === 1) {
  res = matchResult[0][0][0][0](c, async () => { c.res = await this.#notFoundHandler(c) })
  ...
}

// Slow path: multiple handlers → compose them
const composed = compose(matchResult[0], this.errorHandler, this.#notFoundHandler)
const context = await composed(c)
```

`matchResult[0]` is an array of `[[handler, routeRecord], params]` tuples — already ordered by insertion order (i.e., the order you called `app.use` / `app.get`). That order **is** the execution order.

---

## 3. `compose` — the actual chain

**`src/compose.ts` line 15–70**

```ts
export const compose = <E extends Env = Env>(
  middleware: [[Function, unknown], unknown][] | [[Function]][],
  onError?: ErrorHandler<E>,
  onNotFound?: NotFoundHandler<E>
): ((context: Context, next?: Next) => Promise<Context>) => {
  return (context, next) => {
    let index = -1          // tracks last-dispatched position

    return dispatch(0)      // start the chain at slot 0

    async function dispatch(i: number): Promise<Context> {
      if (i <= index) {
        throw new Error('next() called multiple times')   // guard against double-next
      }
      index = i

      let handler
      if (middleware[i]) {
        handler = middleware[i][0][0]        // unwrap [[fn, route], params] → fn
        context.req.routeIndex = i
      } else {
        handler = (i === middleware.length && next) || undefined  // outer next, or undefined
      }

      if (handler) {
        try {
          res = await handler(context, () => dispatch(i + 1))   // ← THIS is next()
        } catch (err) { ... }
      } else {
        if (context.finalized === false && onNotFound) {
          res = await onNotFound(context)   // fell off the end → 404
        }
      }

      if (res && (context.finalized === false || isError)) {
        context.res = res
      }
      return context
    }
  }
}
```

---

## Execution order with `await next()`

Given:
```ts
app.use(async (c, next) => { console.log('A before'); await next(); console.log('A after') })
app.use(async (c, next) => { console.log('B before'); await next(); console.log('B after') })
app.get('/x', (c) => c.text('done'))
```

What happens at runtime:

```
dispatch(0)          → runs middleware A
  A before
  await next()       → dispatch(1)
    B before
    await next()     → dispatch(2)
      route handler runs, sets c.res
    dispatch(2) resolves
    B after
  dispatch(1) resolves
  A after
dispatch(0) resolves → #dispatch returns c.res as the Response
```

The `index` guard at `compose.ts:33` (`if (i <= index) throw`) prevents a middleware from calling `next()` twice — once `dispatch(i)` has been entered, `index` is set to `i`, so any re-entry at the same `i` (or lower) throws immediately.

---

## Summary of the data flow

| Stage | Where | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| `app.use(fn)` | `hono-base.ts` constructor | Calls `#addRoute("ALL", "*", fn)` → `router.add(...)` stores handler in trie |
| `fetch(req)` | `hono-base.ts #dispatch` | `router.match()` returns ordered `[[handler, route], params][]` |
| Composition | `compose.ts:15` | `compose(matchResult[0], ...)` returns a single async fn; called immediately |
| Execution | `compose.ts:32` `dispatch(i)` | Recursively calls handlers; `await next()` is literally `() => dispatch(i+1)` |
| Unwinding | After `dispatch(i+1)` settles | Code after `await next()` in each middleware runs in reverse registration order |
